EXT. DRIVE-IN THEATRE - SNACK BAR PATIO - DUSK

Summer evening, 1962. The screen is a white rectangle against a pink sky. A neon sign says GRAB A BITE. Cars idle in rows. Crickets.

TWO TEENAGERS lean on the rail: BEATRICE "BEEBE" CARRUTHERS (16, ponytail, saddle shoes) and her best friend LOUISE TOLLIVER (16, sunglasses, her father's jacket). Neither looks at the other.

Behind the counter, a SOUR-FACED BOY in a paper hat (17, freckles, glaring at a milkshake machine).

At the window of a nearby station wagon, a MOTHER (40s, curlers under a headscarf) smokes and watches them like a hawk.

BEEBE
He's looking at you.

LOUISE
He's looking at you.

BEEBE
He's looking at her. She's the one with the jacket.

LOUISE
He's looking at the jacket.

The sour-faced boy slides two milkshakes across the counter without a word.

BOY
Four bits.

BEEBE
That's a quarter more than last week.

BOY
Prices go up.

LOUISE
So do tempers.

He flicks his eyes at her, then away. His ears go red.

BEEBE
(to Louise, low)
Ask his name.

LOUISE
You ask his name.

BEEBE
He'd answer you.

LOUISE
He'd answer anyone.

BEEBE
He wouldn't answer me.

Louise sighs. Pushes her sunglasses up into her hair.

LOUISE
What do they call you?

BOY
They don't.

LOUISE
We'll call you something.

BOY
Orville.

BEEBE
Orville?

ORVILLE
It's a family name.

LOUISE
It's a terrible name.

ORVILLE
(unsmiling)
My grandfather flew a biplane in the first war.

BEEBE
Oh!

LOUISE
Oh.

The two girls exchange a glance. Orville slides a napkin across the counter, then another.

ORVILLE (CONT'D)
They're free.

BEEBE
(taking hers)
Thanks, Orville.

The screen flickers. A film leader counts down. The Mother's cigarette glows. She says nothing, but her gaze drifts to the Boy at the counter.

Orville wipes the counter. His hand shakes slightly.

ORVILLE (CONT'D)
(low, to Beebe)
Saturday. If you're free.

BEEBE
She's not free.

LOUISE
She's free.

BEEBE
Louise.

LOUISE
She'd love to.

She nudges the other girl hard. The napkin flutters to the pavement. In the station wagon, the Mother turns away with a small, private smile and flicks ash out the window.

The first scene of the movie begins on the screen, lighting their faces in silver.

FADE OUT.
